        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Roman religion</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">c. 509 BCE – 391 CE</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Origin</span><span class="info-stat-val">the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, shaped by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>, Etruscan and Greek traditions; Roman legend traced most of its institutions to the city’s founders and kings, above all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/numa-pompilius">Numa Pompilius</a></span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Founder or early teachers</span><span class="info-stat-val">none: legend credited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aeneas">Aeneas</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romulus">Romulus</a> and above all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/numa-pompilius">Numa Pompilius</a>, the Sabine second king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, with its rites, calendar and priesthoods</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Scriptures</span><span class="info-stat-val">no scripture: it rested on the correct practice of prayer, rite and sacrifice rather than on doctrine; the Sibylline books were consulted in crises, and calendars, priestly law and writers such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cicero">Cicero</a>, Varro, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livy">Livy</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ovid">Ovid</a> preserve its lore</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Followers today</span><span class="info-stat-val">none: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicene-christianity">Nicene Christianity</a> became the state religion in 380, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodosius-i">Theodosius I</a> ended official support for all other cults in 391; modern groups such as the Associazione Tradizionale Pietas reconstruct its rites</span></div>
