    <section class="kid-tale" aria-labelledby="kid-tale-title">
      <h2 class="kid-tale-title" id="kid-tale-title">The story of Roman religion</h2>
      <ol class="kid-tale-pages"><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/numa-pompilius.jpg" alt="Numa Pompilius" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Numa Pompilius <span class="kid-tale-cr">Classical Numismatic Group, CC BY-SA 2.5</span></figcaption></figure><p>Roman religion was the faith of ancient Rome, in Italy, from more than 2,500 to about 1,600 years ago.</p><p>According to legend, Numa Pompilius, the second king of Rome, gave it its calendar and its priests.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/minerva.jpg" alt="Minerva" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Minerva <span class="kid-tale-cr">AlMare, CC BY-SA 3.0</span></figcaption></figure><p>The Romans worshipped many gods, such as Jupiter, the most powerful, and Minerva.</p><p>They gave the gods prayers and offerings and asked for help in return: “I give that you might give.”</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/julius-caesar.jpg" alt="Julius Caesar" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Julius Caesar <span class="kid-tale-cr">Ángel M. Felicísimo from Mérida, España, Public domain</span></figcaption></figure><p>Rome's priests were usually important men who also ran the government, such as Julius Caesar.</p><p>Six women, the Vestals, tended the sacred fire of the state, and every family honoured its own household gods.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cybele.jpg" alt="Cybele" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Cybele <span class="kid-tale-cr">Marshall Astor, CC BY-SA 2.0</span></figcaption></figure><p>As Rome grew, it welcomed the gods of other peoples instead of getting rid of them.</p><p>Traders and soldiers brought home gods from faraway lands, such as the goddess Cybele and the god Mithras.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aeneid.jpg" alt="Aeneid" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Aeneid <span class="kid-tale-cr">Federico Barocci, Public domain</span></figcaption></figure><p>It had no holy book: what mattered was saying the prayers and doing the rituals exactly right.</p><p>Much of what we know comes from Roman writers, like Vergil, who wrote the Aeneid.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/theodosius-i.jpg" alt="Theodosius I" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Theodosius I <span class="kid-tale-cr">www.livius.org, CC BY-SA 4.0</span></figcaption></figure><p>After Christians had long been persecuted, Christianity became the empire's religion and Emperor Theodosius I ended support for the old gods.</p><p>Yet some old festivals lived on for a long time, and Christian holidays were fitted into the old calendar.</p></li></ol>
    </section>

    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Roman religion was a matter of correct practice rather than belief: prayer, vow and sacrifice were exchanged with the gods on the principle of do ut des, “I give that you might give”.</li><li>Its priests were men of the elite who also held political office, while the six Vestals tended the sacred hearth of the state and every household honoured its own Lares and Penates.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> absorbed the gods of the peoples it ruled and the mystery cults of the East, but its conflict with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> ended in 380, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicene-christianity">Nicene Christianity</a> became the empire’s official religion.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The consul Publius <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claudius">Claudius</a> Pulcher, in 249 BCE, threw the sacred chickens into the sea when they would not eat before a naval battle, “saying that they might drink, since they would not eat”; he was defeated, and his impiety ruined his career.</p></aside>
    </section>

