    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/numa-pompilius"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/numa-pompilius.jpg" alt="Numa Pompilius" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/numa-pompilius">Numa Pompilius</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Numapisocng6371obverse.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Classical Numismatic Group, CC BY-SA 2.5</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>For <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>’s earliest period history and myth are hard to tell apart. In legend the Trojan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aeneas">Aeneas</a>, son of Venus, laid the basis of Roman religion when he brought the Palladium, the Lares and the Penates from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/troy">Troy</a>, and the Greek exile Evander set up the Greatest Altar of Hercules. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romulus">Romulus</a> founded the city after consulting the gods through augury and was credited with the Consualia and the first temple of Jupiter Feretrius. His Sabine successor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/numa-pompilius">Numa Pompilius</a>, who negotiated directly with the gods, was credited with the first calendar, the priesthoods of the Salii, flamines and Vestals, the cults of Jupiter, Mars and Quirinus, and the Temple of Janus. The Etruscan king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarquinius-priscus">Tarquinius Priscus</a> founded a Capitoline temple to Jupiter, Juno and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minerva">Minerva</a>, the model for the highest official cult of the Roman world.</p><p>Roman historians held that the essentials of the Republic’s religion were complete by the end of Numa’s reign. This archaic religion was the foundation of the mos maiorum, “the way of the ancestors”, central to Roman identity. It was open to its neighbours: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> brought practices such as the cult of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apollo">Apollo</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> matched their gods with Greek ones, and Etruscan religion shaped augury. The expulsion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarquinius-superbus">Tarquinius Superbus</a> ended the kingship and began the Republic (509–27 BCE), in which the same men elected to public office also served as augurs and pontiffs.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="beliefs">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Beliefs &amp; Practice</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Roman religion was practical and contractual, resting on the principle do ut des, “I give that you might give”. It depended on knowing and correctly performing prayer, rite and sacrifice, not on faith or dogma. Immortal gods ruled heaven and earth, with gods of the underworld and countless lesser deities. Jupiter, the most powerful, embodied the divine authority of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>’s highest offices. Rome had no native creation myth and matched its deities with the Greek gods, adapting their myths.</p><p>Every sacrifice needed a prayer, and the words had to be exact: a mistake could mean starting a whole festival again. Vows promised offerings in return for benefits, and oaths called on the gods as witnesses. The most potent offering was an animal, usually <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>, sheep or pigs, which had to seem willing; its entrails were reserved for the gods and the meat shared in a communal meal. Public rites took place outdoors at an altar within a templum, a space marked out by augury, and the calendars show roughly forty annual festivals.</p><p>Neglect of the gods was a kind of atheism, while excessive or fearful devotion was superstitio; either could bring divine anger on the state. Beliefs about an afterlife varied, but care of the dead was among the oldest practices: the departed became one of the di Manes, appeased at the February festival of the Parentalia, and tombs were inscribed Dis Manibus, “to the Manes-gods”.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="texts">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Texts &amp; Teachers</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/aeneid"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aeneid.jpg" alt="Aeneid" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/aeneid">Aeneid</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aeneas%27_Flight_from_Troy_by_Federico_Barocci.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Federico Barocci, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Roman religion had no scripture; it lived in rites, calendars, priestly law and oral tradition. In crises the state consulted the Sibylline books, which after the defeat at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-cannae">Cannae</a> in 216 BCE recommended the vow of a ver sacrum and the burial alive of two <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> and two <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gauls">Gauls</a>, and in 206 BCE the cult of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cybele">Magna Mater</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/twelve-tables">Twelve Tables</a> forbade any harmful incantation.</p><p>Much is known through writers. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cicero">Cicero</a>, himself an augur, held religion “necessary” and said the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> considered themselves the most religious of peoples. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livy">Livy</a> recorded the prodigies of the war with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a>, among them a phantom navy in the sky, and the self-sacrifice of Decius Mus. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ovid">Ovid</a>’s Fasti, a poem on the festivals from January to June, mixes antiquarian lore, popular custom and poetic invention. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ennius">Ennius</a> translated Euhemerus, who explained the gods as deified mortals, and Varro saw popular observance as a necessary fiction. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virgil">Vergil</a>’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aeneid">Aeneid</a> gave the Imperial vision of the past, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pliny-the-elder">Pliny the Elder</a> wrote a sceptical history of magic, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apuleius">Apuleius</a>’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-golden-ass">The Golden Ass</a> almost certainly describes the mysteries of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isis">Isis</a>.</p><p>From the 2nd century CE the Church Fathers condemned the empire’s non-Christian religions as “pagan”. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodosius-i">Theodosius I</a> dismantled the old cults, the senator Quintus Aurelius Symmachus protested in a letter, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ambrose">Ambrose</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>, urged the rejection of his plea. Sissel Undheim argues that Mary Beard, John North and Simon Price’s Religions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> dismantled the old story of religious decline in the late Republic.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="spread">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Spread</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/cybele"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cybele.jpg" alt="Cybele" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/cybele">Cybele</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cybele_Getty_Villa_57.AA.19.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Marshall Astor, CC BY-SA 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>As <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> grew, its policy was to absorb the gods and cults of other peoples rather than root them out. In the early Republic it brought Diana from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aricia">Aricia</a> to the Aventine, Castor from Tusculum and Fortuna Primigenia from Praeneste; Camillus promised Juno of Veii a temple if she deserted her city, took Veii in her name and dedicated her temple on the Aventine. In 217 BCE the Venus of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eryx">Eryx</a> came from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punic-wars">Punic Wars</a> (264–146 BCE) generals built many temples to fulfil their vows; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cybele">Magna Mater</a> was brought from Pessinus in 206 BCE and installed on the Palatine in 191, and a mystery cult of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ceres">Ceres</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persephone">Proserpina</a> came in 205 BCE. Troublesome cults were taken over rather than banned: the Senate restricted the Bacchanals in 186 BCE.</p><p>Under the empire, peoples who accepted Rome’s rule kept their own cults, while new foundations by Roman citizens and their allies followed Roman models: newly municipal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sabratha">Sabratha</a> built a Capitolium, and by the middle of the 1st century CE Gaulish Vertault had given up its sacrifice of horses and dogs for a Romanised cult. The Imperial cult developed first in the eastern provinces, then in the West. Soldiers set up altars to their traditional gods, the emperor’s genius and local deities, and traders and legions brought home cults from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>, among them those of Cybele, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isis">Isis</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mithra">Mithras</a> and Sol Invictus; Aurelian (270–275) established an official cult of the Palmyrene Sol Invictus in Rome’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/campus-martius">Campus Martius</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="community">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Community &amp; Society</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/julius-caesar.jpg" alt="Julius Caesar" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Retrato_de_Julio_C%C3%A9sar_(26724093101)_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Ángel M. Felicísimo from Mérida, España, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> had no separate priestly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">caste</a>. Priesthoods were held by the elite, often by the same men who held political office, and a priest drew no stipend. The earliest public priests were probably the flamines; the three major flamines, of Jupiter, Mars and Quirinus, came from patrician families. Pontiffs and augurs advised the magistrates, and the haruspices, an Etruscan profession, read the gods’ will in entrails; the Senate sent noble boys to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/etruria">Etruria</a> to learn the art. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a> became pontifex maximus in 63 BCE, and the emperors held the office after Augustus.</p><p>The six Vestals tended the sacred hearth of the state. A Vestal was freed from her father’s authority and could wield political influence, but one who lost her chastity was buried alive. In each household the paterfamilias was the senior priest, offering daily cult to the Lares and Penates and the family Genius, while his wife cared for the household cult of Vesta. Women attended most festivals, and the rites of the Bona Dea excluded men, but as a rule women did not perform animal sacrifice. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plebeians">Plebeians</a> were at first excluded from the priesthoods; in principle the colleges were later opened to them.</p><p>Rejecting the state religion was tantamount to treason. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> were at times exempt from official sacrifice, but Christians were persecuted: by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nero">Nero</a> after the fire of 64 CE, under Decius’s edict of sacrifice, by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valerian">Valerian</a>, and by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diocletian">Diocletian</a>’s edicts from 303, until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galerius">Galerius</a> revoked the policy in 311.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/theodosius-i"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/theodosius-i.jpg" alt="Theodosius I" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/theodosius-i">Theodosius I</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bust_of_Theodosius_I.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">www.livius.org, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The conversion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-i">Constantine I</a> ended the persecutions; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edict-of-milan">Edict of Milan</a> (313) made mutual toleration the empire’s ideology, and Constantine funded churches and summoned the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-council-of-nicaea">First Council of Nicaea</a>. His nephew Julian, emperor from 361, tried to restore non-Christian practice, but his reforms were reversed after his death in 363. In 380, under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodosius-i">Theodosius I</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicene-christianity">Nicene Christianity</a> became the state religion, and non-Christians faced exclusion from public life or persecution. Gratian refused the office of pontifex maximus and removed the altar of Victory from the Senate house; in 391 Theodosius ended official support for all other cults, put out the Vestals’ sacred fire and vacated their temple.</p><p>Much survived in Christian forms. Christian festivals were fitted into the old calendar, the title divus was later given to saints, the Lupercalia was celebrated as late as the 5th century, and the Council of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tours">Tours</a> forbade Christians to attend the Parentalia in 567. Amulets and binding spells persisted in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> through late antiquity, and charms inherited from classical tradition lived on, reinterpreted, in medieval magic. Patrick Leigh Fermor wrote that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maniots">Maniots</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> abandoned the old religion only towards the end of the 9th century.</p><p>In the 15th century Pomponio Leto’s Roman Academy celebrated the Natalis Romae until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-paul-ii">Pope Paul II</a> dissolved it in 1468, and in the 2000s the Associazione Tradizionale Pietas rebuilt temples and sought legal recognition.</p></div><div class="profile-inv"><p class="profile-inv-k">In the Research Series</p><p class="profile-inv-l"><a href="/essays/faiths-on-the-move">Faiths on the Move<span>Essay</span></a></p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Roman religion was the polytheistic religion of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, an exchange of prayer, vow and sacrifice with the gods that bound together household, city and state. Its priests were the city’s magistrates, and it absorbed the gods of the peoples Rome ruled. After long conflict with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> it lost the state’s support under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodosius-i">Theodosius I</a>, in 380 and 391.</em></p>

